1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,450
Happy New Year!
新年快乐！

2
00:00:04,450 --> 00:00:14,430
For many in the UK, if the New Year means anything, it means starting with a clean slate and turning over a new leaf.
对许多英国人来说，新年若有什么意义，那便是重新开始、改过自新。

3
00:00:14,430 --> 00:00:22,280
And to do this, many of us make New Year's resolutions – a list of ways that we intend to improve ourselves in the year ahead.
为此，我们许多人会制定新年决心——一份计划在来年提升自我的清单。

4
00:00:22,280 --> 00:00:27,900
We reflect on our past transgressions and resolve not to do them again.
我们反思过去的过错，决心不再重犯。

5
00:00:27,900 --> 00:00:36,190
Refraining from smoking is ever popular, as is a vow to take up more exercise or spend more time with family.
戒烟向来很受欢迎，发誓要更多地锻炼身体或花更多的时间陪伴家人也同样常见。

6
00:00:36,190 --> 00:00:40,790
But where does the idea of a New Year's resolution come from?
但新年决心的想法源自何处呢？

7
00:00:40,790 --> 00:00:48,050
The answer might lie 4,000 years ago in ancient Babylonia, according to History.com.
据History.com网站记载，答案或许能追溯到4000年前的古代巴比伦。

8
00:00:48,050 --> 00:00:54,040
The Babylonians are believed to be the first to hold recorded celebrations for the New Year.
人们认为巴比伦人是最早有记载庆祝新年的民族。

9
00:00:54,040 --> 00:01:07,000
The 12-day-long celebration, Akitu, allowed them not only to reaffirm their loyalty to the king, or crown a new one, but also to pledge to the gods to pay their debts and return borrowed objects.
这场长达12天的庆典名为“Akitu”，在庆典中，他们不仅会重申对国王的忠诚（或为新国王加冕），还会向诸神发誓偿还债务、归还借来的物品。

10
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:17,800
For their 'good behaviour', they believed the gods would bestow favour upon them for the year to come. If not, there would be divine consequences!
他们相信，凭借这种“良好行为”，诸神会在来年赐福于他们；否则，将面临神的惩罚！

11
00:01:17,800 --> 00:01:21,140
The Romans too had similar traditions.
罗马人也有类似的传统。

12
00:01:21,140 --> 00:01:30,800
New Year's Day was a time when senators would swear they had upheld the laws and city leaders and soldiers would take an oath of loyalty to the emperor.
新年当天，参议员会宣誓维护法律，城市官员和士兵会宣誓效忠皇帝。

13
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:36,240
Incidentally, the month of January, is named after the Roman god Janus.
顺便说一句，一月（January）以罗马神雅努斯（Janus）命名。

14
00:01:36,240 --> 00:01:50,310
He is the god of beginnings, transitions and time, among other things and is depicted as having two faces – one looking to the past and the other facing forward to the future.
雅努斯是开端、过渡和时间等等之神，被描绘为有两张脸——一张回望过去，另一张展望未来。

15
00:01:50,310 --> 00:01:55,430
The Romans may be long gone, but their legacy lives on.
罗马人早已消逝，但他们的传统留存至今。

16
00:01:55,430 --> 00:02:09,890
Come the turn of the year, our eyes turn inwards. We look at self-improvement, review our past mistakes, confess our wrongdoings, seek atonement and try to sharpen up in general.
岁末年初，我们开始向内审视：关注自我提升，回顾过往错误，坦陈过失，寻求赎罪，并努力全面改进。

17
00:02:09,890 --> 00:02:20,150
Ultimately, much like the Babylonians and Romans, we seek to reaffirm the best parts of ourselves, while leaving the dross behind.
最终，和巴比伦人、罗马人一样，我们试图肯定自身最美好的部分，同时摒弃糟粕。

18
00:02:20,150 --> 00:02:30,790
Whether you're looking at a quick behavioural tweak, or a huge personality overhaul, you're taking part in a tradition that goes back centuries.
无论你是想快速改善行为，还是彻底重塑性格，你都在参与一项可追溯数百年的传统。

19
00:02:30,790 --> 00:02:34,780
I wish you all the best sticking to your resolutions.
祝愿大家都能坚持自己的决心。

20
00:02:34,780 --> 00:02:36,560
Happy New Year!
新年快乐！
